{
  "lead": {
    "text": "Look closely at how an accompaniment is made, then follow it from the pan to its place in a meal.",
    "role": "original editorial invitation"
  },
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "id": "opening",
      "text": "A dish name is a starting point, not a complete description. To understand a bowl of ou khatta, ask what happens before the fruit reaches the pan, what joins it there, and how it is served. Those questions let us compare choices without choosing a single version as the standard.",
      "source_ids": [],
      "role": "original editorial framing"
    },
    {
      "id": "sweet",
      "text": "In her 2017 sweet elephant-apple relish, Sasmita cooks and drains the fruit before moving it to another pan. Cumin and mustard seeds temper the oil; ginger, jaggery or sugar, and coconut follow. She also allows the cooked pieces to be crushed. Even within this recipe, texture is a choice.[^1]",
      "source_ids": [
        "food-ou-sasmita2017"
      ],
      "role": "attributed source paraphrase"
    },
    {
      "id": "paste",
      "text": "Her 2019 sweet-and-sour version uses a mustard–cumin paste alongside panch phoron in the oil. She blends the paste separately; the whole spices go into hot oil before the cooked fruit is added. The precooked, drained fruit then meets the paste and jaggery. She suggests rice and dalma alongside it. These are two recipes by the same cook, offering variation rather than evidence from two households.[^2]",
      "source_ids": [
        "food-ou-sasmita2019"
      ],
      "role": "attributed source paraphrase",
      "shared_source_qualification": {
        "source_id": "food-ou-sasmita2017",
        "text": "These are two recipes by the same cook, offering variation rather than evidence from two households.",
        "charged_words": 16
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "comparison",
      "text": "Reading a method this way changes the comparison. An ingredient list tells us what is present; the sequence tells us what the cook does with it. When asking someone about their version, “What goes into the pan first?” can open a more useful conversation than “Which version is correct?”",
      "source_ids": [],
      "role": "original editorial framing"
    },
    {
      "id": "memory",
      "text": "Julie Acharya Ray’s 2014 memoir places ou khatta in her mother’s Kartik meal. On banana leaves, rice and dal occupy the centre, with vegetable dishes and ou khatta around them. This is one family’s remembered arrangement, not a menu for every household.[^3]",
      "source_ids": [
        "food-ou-ray2014"
      ],
      "role": "attributed source paraphrase"
    },
    {
      "id": "closing",
      "text": "The recipe and the remembered meal answer different questions. One follows the work of making an accompaniment; the other returns it to the food eaten beside it. Read them together, and there is more to ask about a small bowl than its name: how it was prepared, who prepared it, and what else was on the plate.",
      "source_ids": [],
      "role": "original editorial framing"
    }
  ],
  "notes": [
    {
      "number": "1",
      "text": "Sasmita, [Meetha Oou Khatta](https://www.firsttimercook.com/meetha-oou-khatta-sweet-elephant-apple-relish/), First Timer Cook, 10 December 2017; updated 28 April 2021. Method and notes.",
      "source_id": "food-ou-sasmita2017"
    },
    {
      "number": "2",
      "text": "Sasmita, [Khata Meetha Oou](https://www.firsttimercook.com/khata-meetha-oou-sweet-sour-elephant-apple-relish/), First Timer Cook, 4 January 2019; updated 28 April 2021. Method and serving suggestion.",
      "source_id": "food-ou-sasmita2019"
    },
    {
      "number": "3",
      "text": "Julie Acharya Ray, [The Month of Kartik in my Mother’s Kitchen](https://www.saveur.com/article/travels/kartik-holy-month/), Saveur, 9 October 2014. Family memoir.",
      "source_id": "food-ou-ray2014"
    }
  ],
  "source_limits": [
    "Two recipes are one author’s work, not independent household coverage.",
    "Memoir supports one remembered family meal only.",
    "No recipe quantities, timings, testing, health, temple-wide rule, origin or district distribution claimed.",
    "Unresolved university Assam/Odisha reference remains outside this article."
  ],
  "rights": "Paraphrase and concise attribution only; no recipe or memoir photographs copied; no claim that source prose is openly licensed."
}

